doOpT -- SEQ 591 (Tony, 2026-11-10): Do as an operator, WHILe's shape (SEQ 580). Do declares op="doLoop" and its hand action
is deleted; the default action OPERATOr builds its instruct from its label, the statement then the condition, the order opDo
reads. doT (both roads) must not move; these rows pin what the default CHANGES and the controls it must not. Rows prefixed
DP-. Interpreted only.

DP-1  the body slot refuses: today "REFUSED Do -- its statement handed up no instruct" (Do's own refusal, pinned by no row
      until now); the default names the slot by its label and, the body having handed up nothing, says so.
DP-2  the condition slot refuses: today F-147's attach refusal naming the internal list (doArms); the default names the slot
      by its label.
DP-3  control: a bare-name condition is a value, not an instruct, and is not refused; two passes, so the test is read.
DP-4  control: the old road's Do, a refused body in an action: refused naming xl1, the rest of the action does not run.
DP-5  control (R0): top level through FILe, which runs as it builds -- no testOP; the statement after the Do runs.
DP-6  control (R0): a new-road action body (dpN, built by dpNew -- whileT's whNew), a break inside the Do ends only the Do.
      A "parse failed" line would mean dpN ran on the old road; the refusals column would show it.

Not run alone: pop.sh appends the row, print "NEXT", a sentinel; one process per row. Each row reads exit and sentinel; stdout
(lines joined by |); and the lines that are REFUSED naming Do, doArms or xl1, or say parse failed, joined by |.

